<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0458">Remark <span data-tag="0458">8.2.4.2</span>.</a> In the situation of Definition <a data-tag="0456" href="/tag/0456">8.2.4.1</a>, if the diagram (<a data-tag="0457" href="/tag/0457">8.41</a>) exhibits $\lambda $ as represented by $G$ if and only if the functor $\widetilde{G}$ carries each isomorphism in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{+}$ (regarded as an object of the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{Tw}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{+})$) to a universal object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. This follows from Remark <a data-tag="043Z" href="/tag/043Z">8.2.1.2</a>, since every isomorphism in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{+}$ is isomorphic to an identity morphism (when viewed as an object of $\operatorname{Tw}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{+})$). </p>
